 "findings": [
  "The previous rule (all terms, Jaccard 0.25, two shared) left 683 clusters for 781 posts: precision 0.945, recall 0.015",
  "Informative terms, Jaccard 0.15, three shared: precision 0.957, recall 0.058, 539 clusters; adopted as the default",
  "The overlap coefficient against a cluster's growing term set joins nearly everything (precision 0.026) and is rejected",
  "Recall stays low under every rule: comments under one story share few words, so lexical clustering finds tight groups, not whole discussions",
  "Adding a general-word list to the informative filter (after a live matcha run surfaced refinements such as 'people who', 'feel like', 'know') moved the default from precision 0.973 / recall 0.055 to precision 0.957 / recall 0.058; it was kept because the live hypotheses lost the generic refinements while product phrases stayed. The general-word list is a hand-written English list, not learned",
  "Re-measured after content terms dropped other languages' function words, @handles and bare domains and read unspaced CJK as script words: default precision 0.9573, recall 0.0575 (from 0.9574 and 0.0576); the Hacker News sample is English, so this checks those changes cost nothing here, not that they help",
  "With measured background-common words excluded from informative terms, the default reaches precision 0.9648 and recall 0.0598 (from 0.9573 and 0.0575). Not an independent result: the word list was derived partly from these same Hacker News captures (research/commerce/background-common-words.json)"
 ]
